###  http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind.forward
<http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind.forward> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                <http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind> ;
                                                       rdfs:comment "Delivered or Imported as defined 619682.Forward Active Energy is a positive kWh value as one would naturally expect to find as energy is supplied by the utility and consumed at the service.Forward Reactive Energy is a positive VArh value as one would naturally expect to find in the presence of inductive loading.In polyphase metering the forward energy register is incremented when the sum of the phase energies is greater than zeroimg srcHTS1.PNG width209 height16 border0 altgraphic" ;
                                                       rdfs:label "forward"@en .


###  http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind.lagging
<http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind.lagging> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                <http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind> ;
                                                       rdfs:comment "Typically used to describe that a power factor is lagging the reference value. Note 1 When used to describe VA lagging describes a form of measurement where reactive power is considered in all four quadrants but real power is considered only in quadrants I and IV.Note 2 When used to describe power factor the term Lagging implies that the PF is negative. The term lagging in this case takes the place of the negative sign. If a signed PF value is to be passed by the data producer then the direction of flow enumeration zero none should be used in order to avoid the possibility of creating an expression that employs a double negative. The data consumer should be able to tell from the sign of the data if the PF is leading or lagging. This principle is analogous to the concept that Reverse energy is an implied negative value and to publish a negative reverse value would be ambiguous.Note 3 Lagging power factors typically indicate inductive loading." ;
                                                       rdfs:label "lagging"@en .


###  http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind.leading
<http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind.leading> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                <http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind> ;
                                                       rdfs:comment "Typically used to describe that a power factor is leading the reference value.Note Leading power factors typically indicate capacitive loading." ;
                                                       rdfs:label "leading"@en .


###  http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind.net
<http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind.net> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                            <http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind> ;
                                                   rdfs:comment "Forward  Reverse See 619682.Note In some systems the value passed as a net value could become negative. In other systems the value passed as a net value is always a positive number and rollsover and rollsunder as needed. " ;
                                                   rdfs:label "net"@en .


###  http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind.none
<http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind.none> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                             <http://www.iec.ch/TC57/CIM#FlowDirectionKind> ;
                                                    rdfs:comment "Not Applicable NA" ;
                                                    rdfs:label "none"@en .


